Out on the bridge wing the second mate has finished the forenoon watch, and the chart has to show the watch's work before the master comes up to look at it.

The picture is one corner of the working chart, drawn with north to the top of the sheet, ruled all over in squares of one nautical mile, with the passage leg drawn on it as a single straight track line. The chart is what a mate would hand over: pencil work on printed paper, nothing tidied away.

What the watch has to work from, all of it given:

  - the leg runs 045 true out of waypoint WP12, and the whole watch is on that one leg;
  - the ship makes good 6.0 knots through the water on that course;
  - the watch is worked hourly from 0800 to 1300, so there are six hours in all;
  - the tidal atlas gives one stream over the whole of this leg for the whole of this watch: it sets 315 true and runs at 2.0 knots, and it does not change;
  - the chart's squares are one nautical mile on a side.

Every hour carries two marks and one line. The reckoned position for that hour is a small cross, the position observed at that same hour is a small circle, and a short straight line joins the cross to the circle. The hour itself is lettered beside its own pair in four figures, 0800 through to 1300, so that the pairs cannot be confused with one another.

Where each observed position falls is the watch's work and I am deliberately not telling you: neither the direction of any of those six short lines nor how far it reaches. Apart from the six four-figure times, nothing else is written on the chart: no note of the stream, no arrow labelled with a bearing, no figures in knots or in miles. Keep the six pairs well apart along the track and draw each cross, circle and joining line large enough to be made out one from another.
